A few more AI math and science results to add to the list from this week: 

@AnthropicAI says Claude also disproved the Exponential Time Hypothesis, separately from OpenAI. It found the key algorithm on Oct. 16 using "14M output tokens and no human input."

@axiommathai’s AI agents computer-checked a 15,000-page math proof, written by ~100 mathematicians over decades, in 24 days. They also caught a flaw in one of the original arguments.

@psipbc says its AI made progress on five open quantum physics problems, each backed by a paper and a computer-checked proof.

@johncrickett Across every company I work with, most engineers' time goes toward evaluating code written by agents.

That's the bottleneck, for sure, but I don't think the right answer is to spend more time doing manual code reviews.

I don't think a single answer works for everyone, but we've had success building automated review processes.

@signulll it's creating a new abstraction that's a bit bigger than window management

basically it's task based and the apps/websites needed for that task are available easily, others cause you to switch to a different mode to use them

notifications can popup from other modes if the classifier thinks they're important

@pauliusztin_ The key here is in the context, I think.

Basically, you want the evaluator to use a separate context from the agent that writes the code.

They could both be the same exact model. They could both be the same harness. But one should have the info required to write the code and the other info to verify it.

